2nd year students of BME into the field of metallic structures engineering. First part of this course focuses on understanding fundamental nature and key properties of metallic alloys and its phase transformation mechanisms which underlie mechanical and chemical strength behaviour, as well as microstructure and crystallography.
In the second topic, the mixtures made up of metal matrix composites are analysed in order to study their fatigue behaviour both from experimental investigations on testing machines as well as from numerical methods (such as FEA). In addition physical and modelling laws for predicting the interfacial behavior is also given.
At the end of the subject, students know the following:
1. Fundamental knowledge about the properties of metallic substances and their phase transformation;
2.Fundamental knowledge about mixed metallic structures and their performance characteristics;
3.General theoretical models used for simulation analysis of metallic materials and alloys;
4.Comprehensive knowledge about metallic chemical components’ role into interfacial phenomena, deformation behaviors and failure mechanisms of metallic structures.
